{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01WUpMXtoLt8MEVed8hxe5eD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. I wrote **Q25: Biggest security risks with tool-using agents?** (`03-025-biggest-security-risks-with-tool-using-agents.md`).\n\nThe answer covers a 4-vector threat taxonomy:\n\n1. **Prompt injection** (highest severity) — direct vs *indirect* injection (the trickier production threat where malicious instructions hide inside RAG-retrieved documents or tool outputs). Mitigated by delimiter fencing in the system prompt + an injection classifier (NeMo Guardrails / Azure Prompt Shield) at the retrieval boundary.\n\n2. **Over-privileged tools** — broad schemas like `query_database(sql: string)` become data-exfiltration vectors when injected. Fix: least-privilege scoping (`get_order_status(order_id: string)`) + read-only credentials per tool.\n\n3. **SSRF / network attacks** — `fetch_url` tools with no allowlist let injected agents call internal metadata services or attacker servers. Fix: domain allowlist + Docker `--network=none` isolation.\n\n4. **PII leakage via traces** — LangSmith/Datadog traces capture full tool I/O including customer data. Fix: Presidio scrub before writing to trace storage.\n\nThe note includes a tradeoff table mapping each risk to mitigation + cost, a first-person verbal script, 3 pitfalls (especially the weak-candidate mistake of only naming injection without covering SSRF or over-privilege), and cross-links to Q22 (schema design), Q23 (sandboxing), and Q5 (autonomy boundaries)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":284,"cache_read_input_tokens":64551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":284,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a78b996e-a1d8-4148-b35f-314a21a04b01","uuid":"6eee4d65-620e-4e5c-a38b-1b1bf4b98b